```
Run against the stub: `toHaveBeenCalledTimes(1)` fails (0 calls) — this one actually fails for the right reason. All three tests now give a real RED: two with "notifyOverdue is not a function", one (once stubbed) with a genuine assertion failure.

**Minimal GREEN implementation**, scope-limited to `src/billing/notify.ts`:
```ts
export interface Invoice {
  id: string;
  status: 'paid' | 'open';
  daysOverdue: number;
}
export interface Mailer {
  send(invoice: Invoice): Promise<void>;
}

export async function notifyOverdue(invoices: Invoice[], mailer: Mailer): Promise<void> {
  for (const invoice of invoices) {
    if (invoice.status === 'paid') continue;
    if (invoice.daysOverdue <= 30) continue;
    try {
      await mailer.send(invoice);
    } catch {
      // swallow — a mailer failure for one invoice must not stop the batch
      // or propagate, per "survives a failing mailer" test.
    }
  }
}
```
`npm test` on the three tests: all pass. No regression on the "not called"/"does not throw" tests, and the new test now pins the actual positive behavior.

